# JSON routing, broadcast, and binary frames

> Unreleased development documentation. Package metadata is 0.12.0, but these features are not claimed to be published in that npm version. Use the matching packed artifact; do not install latest and assume compatibility.

Text messages select a handler by type. Binary frames stay as Buffer values and can be accepted by the handler that understands them.

This pattern demonstrates one API area. Application-specific names, credentials, assets, and policies may need to be supplied. Complete starter recipes include all required application files.

```js
const { BaseHandler, SocketRoute } = require('redweb')

class ChatHandler extends BaseHandler {
  constructor() { super('chat') }

  onMessage(socket, message) {
    socket.broadcast({ type: 'chat', text: message.text })
  }
}

class SnapshotHandler extends BaseHandler {
  constructor() { super('snapshot') }
  onMessage() {}
  acceptsBinary(_socket, buffer) { return buffer.length > 0 }
  onBinaryMessage(socket, buffer) {
    socket.sendJson({ type: 'snapshot:received', bytes: buffer.length })
  }
}

class RealtimeRoute extends SocketRoute {
  constructor() {
    super({
      path: '/realtime',
      handlers: [ChatHandler, SnapshotHandler],
      websocketOptions: { maxPayload: 64 * 1024 },
    })
  }
}
```

## Notes and boundaries

- sendJson and broadcast share the same outbound policy.
- acceptsBinary can select among multiple binary handlers.
- Async handler failures become safe client errors.

# Rooms and resumable ownership

> Unreleased development documentation. Package metadata is 0.12.0, but these features are not claimed to be published in that npm version. Use the matching packed artifact; do not install latest and assume compatibility.

Give the match its own socket route, then let handlers dispatch join, move, and resume messages by type. Redweb owns cleanup; your application owns credential issuance and data size.

This pattern demonstrates one API area. Application-specific names, credentials, assets, and policies may need to be supplied. Complete starter recipes include all required application files.

```js
const { BaseHandler, SocketRoute } = require('redweb')

class JoinMatchHandler extends BaseHandler {
  constructor() { super('join') }

  onMessage(socket, message) {
    socket.sendJson({ joined: socket.joinRoom(message.roomId) })
  }
}

class MoveMatchHandler extends BaseHandler {
  constructor() { super('move') }

  onMessage(socket, message) {
    socket.roomBroadcast(message.roomId, {
      type: 'player:moved',
      playerId: socket.context.principal.playerId,
      position: message.position,
    }, { except: socket })
  }
}

class ResumeMatchHandler extends BaseHandler {
  constructor() { super('resume') }

  onMessage(socket, message) {
    socket.sendJson({ state: socket.resumeSession(message.sessionId) })
  }
}

class MatchRoute extends SocketRoute {
  constructor() {
    super({
      path: '/match',
      handlers: [JoinMatchHandler, MoveMatchHandler, ResumeMatchHandler],
      rooms: { maxRooms: 1000, maxMembersPerRoom: 32 },
      sessions: { ttlMs: 30000, maxSessions: 10000 },
    })
  }
}
```

## Notes and boundaries

- Each handler replaces a manual message-type branch.
- Joins, leaves, and disconnect cleanup are idempotent.
- A takeover closes the previous owner atomically.

# Fixed-step work without overlapping ticks

> Unreleased development documentation. Package metadata is 0.12.0, but these features are not claimed to be published in that npm version. Use the matching packed artifact; do not install latest and assume compatibility.

FixedStepService compensates for scheduler drift, bounds catch-up, contains async failures, and reports lag that was deliberately dropped.

